  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1029 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nOutside of passing high clouds associated with a shortwave \nnorth of the area this evening, the weather remains quiet and \ndry. Skies clear out tonight helping temperatures to cool down \ninto the 40s by Tuesday morning. Mostly sunny skies start the \nday as upper-level ridging begins to build across the Great \nPlains. Afternoon temperatures should approach the upper 60s and\nlow 70s ahead of our next disturbance. Another shortwave trough\nwill lift north from the southern Rockies late Tuesday \nafternoon. Increasing clouds are anticipated Tuesday evening in \nresponse. A few showers may develop over portions of northern \nKansas and southeastern Nebraska with the passage of this \ntrough. These showers will struggle to produce much if any \nrainfall as a dry sub-cloud layer should prevent much of the \nprecipitation from reaching the ground. Increased cloud cover \nhelps to keep overnight temperatures a touch warmer with Wednesday\nmorning lows in the upper 40s and low 50s.\n\nWednesday afternoon through Friday morning turns back to warm, \ndry, and quiet weather as large-scale ridging sets up across \nthe central CONUS. Other than scattered high clouds at times, \nmostly sunny skies and light winds are anticipated. Afternoon \ntemperatures climb well into the 70s with overnight low \ntemperatures mostly in the 50s. The quiet weather comes to an \nend by Friday afternoon as our next weather system enters the \npicture.\n\nFriday afternoon remains warm, but a deep trough over the\nSouthwest begins to eject northeast across the Great Plains\nduring this time. In response, lee troughing commences to the \nwest of the region and the surface pressure gradient tightens. \nStrong south winds overspread the area as a result with peak \ngusts of 30 to 40 mph possible. This will pose increased fire \ndanger concerns with dry standing crops waiting to be harvested.\nThat being said, increasing relative humidity as moisture \nreturns to the area may help to limit these concerns. With the \nstrong southerly winds, temperatures remain well into the 70s. \n\nAs the aforementioned trough lifts northeast, a cold front\nswings across the region on Saturday. Our next chance of rain \naccompanies this front as well. Currently, Saturday afternoon \nsees the cold front bisect the region by afternoon. Cooler \ntemperatures in the 60s are expected behind the front over \nnortheast Nebraska with warmer temperatures in the 70s across \nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa. Our highest rain chances \nwill be during this time. Most locations have a 40-70% chance of\nshowers and with perhaps an isolated rumble of thunder.\n \nTemperatures turn cooler on Sunday as the front mostly clears \nthe region with 60s back in the forecast. A least a low chance \n(20-40%) of showers remains in the forecast as the trough \nmoves overhead. The cooler weather continues into next week \nwith additional rain chances possible with a more unsettled \npattern appearing likely. The exact details differ between \nforecast models, but these details should become clearer as the \nweek progresses.",
